1. A method of encoding video information, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, video data comprising a plurality of coding units of a current picture;

determining, for a current coding unit of the plurality of coding units, prediction information associated with the current coding unit, the prediction information including at least one of an intra prediction mode, an inter prediction mode, motion information, reference picture information, partition information, or transform information;

obtaining neighboring coding unit data associated with one or more neighboring coding units adjacent to or otherwise spatially or temporally related to the current coding unit, the neighboring coding unit data including prediction information of the one or more neighboring coding units;

deriving, based at least in part on the neighboring coding unit data, candidate prediction information for the current coding unit;

comparing the prediction information determined for the current coding unit with the candidate prediction information derived from the neighboring coding unit data;

generating a match/mismatch flag indicating whether the prediction information of the current coding unit matches the candidate prediction information;

encoding the match/mismatch flag into a bitstream;

in response to the match/mismatch flag indicating a match, omitting from the bitstream at least a portion of the prediction information for the current coding unit and causing a decoder to derive the omitted portion from the neighboring coding unit data; and

in response to the match/mismatch flag indicating a mismatch, encoding into the bitstream difference information or explicit prediction information identifying the prediction information for the current coding unit relative to the candidate prediction information.

2. The method of claim 1, wherein the neighboring coding unit data comprises data from at least one of a left neighboring coding unit, an above neighboring coding unit, an above-left neighboring coding unit, an above-right neighboring coding unit, a co-located coding unit in a reference picture, or a previously encoded coding unit.

3. The method of claim 1, wherein deriving the candidate prediction information comprises selecting a most probable prediction mode from prediction modes of the one or more neighboring coding units.

4. The method of claim 1, wherein the match/mismatch flag comprises a binary syntax element indicating whether a prediction mode of the current coding unit is identical to a prediction mode derived from the neighboring coding unit data.

5. The method of claim 1, wherein the difference information comprises an index identifying one of a plurality of candidate prediction modes generated using the neighboring coding unit data.

6. The method of claim 1, further comprising context-adaptively entropy encoding the match/mismatch flag using a context determined based on at least one characteristic of the neighboring coding unit data.

7. The method of claim 1, wherein the prediction information comprises motion vector information, and wherein the match/mismatch flag indicates whether a motion vector predictor derived from the neighboring coding unit data matches a motion vector associated with the current coding unit.

8. The method of claim 1, wherein the prediction information comprises intra prediction direction information, and wherein the match/mismatch flag indicates whether an intra prediction direction of the current coding unit matches an intra prediction direction predicted from the neighboring coding unit data.

9. The method of claim 1, further comprising reconstructing the current coding unit based on the prediction information and residual information, and storing reconstructed data of the current coding unit for use as neighboring coding unit data for encoding a subsequent coding unit.

10. A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause the one or more processors to perform the method of claim 1.
